title: "Rooftop Solar Site Survey Checklist | HelioLattice"
description: "A rooftop solar site survey checklist covering roof, shading, electrical, photos and verdict, so quotations rest on facts and rework drops."
crumbs:
  - name: Guides
    href: /guides/
  - name: Site survey checklist
eyebrow: Guide · Survey
h1: A rooftop solar site survey checklist that prevents rework.
lead: Most quotation errors begin at the survey. A survey that records the same facts every time, with photos, gives the quotation team something solid to price and the installation team something solid to follow.
answer: Record the roof, the shade, the electrical connection and the access, attach categorised photos, and end with a clear verdict. Make the surveyor, date and location part of the record.
published: 2026-10-11
sections:
  - h: Before you leave for the site
    p:
      - Confirm the address, contact person and the best time to visit. Open the enquiry so that what you record attaches to it.
      - Carry the same checklist every time. Consistency lets the quotation team compare surveys without guessing what was skipped.
  - h: The roof
    list:
      - Roof type and condition, such as concrete, sheet or tile
      - Usable area, with obstructions marked
      - Orientation and tilt
      - Parapet, water tank, staircase head room and other structures
      - Load-bearing comfort for the mounting structure
  - h: Shade and sun
    list:
      - Nearby buildings, trees and poles that can shade the array
      - Times of day when shade falls on the usable area
      - Photos taken from the proposed array position
  - h: The electrical side
    list:
      - Sanction load, connection type and phase
      - Meter location and condition
      - Distribution board position and spare capacity
      - Earthing condition and cable routes between roof and board
  - h: Access and logistics
    list:
      - How material gets to the roof, such as stairs, lift or crane
      - Safe working conditions on the roof
      - Parking and storage for material at the site
  - h: Photos that help later
    p:
      - Photos are the survey's evidence. Group them by what they show so someone can find the meter photo or the shade photo without scrolling through a gallery.
    list:
      - Roof overview and each section
      - Shade sources
      - Meter and distribution board
      - Earthing points
      - Access route
    after: Capture the location of the visit with the survey, so there is proof that the surveyor reached the right site.
  - h: End with a verdict
    p:
      - A survey is only useful if it ends in a decision. Record one of three outcomes, so the next team knows what to do.
    list:
      - Go, the site is suitable as surveyed
      - Conditional go, the site works if named conditions are met
      - No go, with the reason
takeaways:
  - Use the same checklist on every survey.
  - Group photos by what they show.
  - Finish with go, conditional go or no go.
